{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>You shall not round the corner of your head.</b> Scripture warned against shaving the two temples, whether on their own without shaving the head, or together with shaving the head at once. If he shaved both, he receives two sets of lashes, for each temple separately." "<b>And you shall not destroy the corner of your beard.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat), the reason for the prohibition is so as not to nullify the sign that the Holy One, blessed be He, imprinted upon the male species, to distinguish it from the female species; for one who does so is acting counter to the Name, like one who sows mixed seeds (kil'ayim), and in everything He made in the work of Creation He wrote concerning them 'after its kind' (למינהו). And had He said 'your beard' (את זקנך), the destruction of the entire beard would be under one prohibition; but since He said 'the corner of' (את פאת), and there are five corners in the beard — two on the right and two on the left, and one below, which is the place of the beard between the joints (פרקי) — if he shaved and destroyed all of these he is liable to five sets of lashes; therefore it says 'corner' (פאת), to make him liable for each and every corner." "<b>And by way of</b> reason (sekhel), the reason for this commandment is that all of a person's actions are drawn after the five senses within him, and it is not fitting to eliminate them from their function, for he cannot live without them; rather, it is fitting for him to limit himself with them to what is permitted and necessary, and to leave some of them for the needs of his body's sustenance. Therefore He forbade the destruction with a blade (תער) in five corners, corresponding to the five senses, and this is a warning and admonition to a person, hinting that it is impossible for him to remove the five senses entirely, for he would be destroying his body and ruining himself. But shaving with scissors (מספרים) was permitted, to leave some hair, and this is not called destruction — corresponding to the fact that it is fitting to leave the function of the senses in part, so that he may live and be sustained through them." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah, the reason for the prohibition of destruction is that the letters of the Havayah (הוי״ה) of the hidden Name are inscribed in the pattern of the body — the yod (י״ו) and the heh (ה״א) and the vav (ופ״ו) in circumcision and in the mouth and in the tongue, and this is the reason for the prohibition against destroying the upper beard and the lower beard, for the prohibition is equal in both. And contemplate, O El, wonders: for so the natural philosophers wrote in their books that places which are equal in the growth of hair have an affinity and kinship between them, and tendons (גידין) extend from this place to that place, and when hair grows in this place it grows in that place. And for this reason women and eunuchs have no beard, because this affinity is lacking in them, and they do not have tendons extending from this place to that place. And since we find in these two places equivalence and unity — and behold, they are a sign and figure of unity — our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said (Shabbat 86a): 'A person should always sanctify himself at the time of relations.' And the commandment is a warning against erasing the letters of the Name, and this is clear."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:27:12.485117517Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}